WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Check the chart label. Before interpreting a chart, identify the instrument, the dates shown, and the time interval represented by each mark.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:44.000
One period, four prices. A daily candle summarizes one trading day. An hourly candle summarizes one hour of prices.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Zoom changes the picture. A short chart may show a sharp move. A longer view can reveal that the move is small relative to earlier changes.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Axes set the scale. Read the price values on the vertical axis. A narrow displayed range can make a modest move look dramatic.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Compare equal intervals. A series of candles is only comparable after checking that the interval stays the same throughout the view.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Gaps can appear. A new period can open above or below the previous close. The chart may show a gap between those prices.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Past does not predict. A chart is a record of earlier transactions. A visual pattern alone cannot promise the next price.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Read the context. State the timeframe, start and end dates, and observed price range before offering an interpretation.
